}
}
```

A Dog object and a Cat object are child-class objects because both classes extend Animal. The reference variable used to access these objects can be declared using either the child class or the parent class. The choice affects which methods are accessible through that reference.

Answer: Tight coupling means using a child-class reference to refer to an object of that same child class. For example:

```java
Dog d = new Dog();
d.ownerComesHome();
```

Here, Dog is the reference type, and the object is also created using Dog. Because the reference is declared as Dog, the compiler allows access to the methods available through the Dog type, including methods specifically declared in Dog. This approach is useful when the program specifically needs to work with a particular child-class type. However, it makes the reference dependent on that specific type, reducing flexibility when the program needs to work with different child classes through a common parent type.

Answer: Consider these declarations:

```java
Dog d = new Dog();
Cat c = new Cat();
```

The reference d refers to a Dog object, while c refers to a Cat object. Each reference is tied to its declared child type. If a program needs to work with dogs, cats, and parrots, it may require separate reference variables and separate statements for each type. This makes the program less flexible when new child classes are introduced. A common parent-class reference provides a more flexible way to work with objects belonging to different child classes.

