Output: Dog welcomes the owner. Cat responds to the owner. The reference variable ref is declared once using the parent type Animal. It first refers to a Dog object and then to a Cat object. The same method call executes the implementation belonging to the actual object. This demonstrates runtime polymorphism.

Answer: Loose coupling allows a parent reference to refer to objects of different child classes. Suppose Animal is the parent class, and Dog, Cat, and Parrot are child classes. The same reference can refer to each of these objects at different times: Animal ref;

```java
ref = new Dog();
ref.ownerComesHome();
ref = new Cat();
ref.ownerComesHome();
ref = new Parrot();
ref.ownerComesHome();
```

Assuming each child class overrides ownerComesHome(), the same method call produces behavior specific to the current object. This is runtime polymorphism. The reference type remains Animal, but the actual object determines which overridden method implementation executes.

Answer: Method overriding occurs when a child class provides its own implementation of an inherited instance method using a compatible method signature. For example:

```java
class Animal {
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Animal responds to the owner.");
}
}
class Dog extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog welcomes the owner.");
}
}
```

The Dog class overrides the ownerComesHome() method inherited from Animal. When the method is called through an Animal reference that refers to a Dog object, Java executes the overridden implementation in Dog. The @Override annotation helps the compiler check that the method correctly overrides an inherited method.

Answer: A parent-class reference can refer to different child-class objects, but the methods directly accessible through the reference are determined by its declared type. For example:

```java
class Animal {
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Animal responds to the owner.");
}
}
class Dog extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog welcomes the owner.");
}
```

void fetchBall() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog fetches the ball.");
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Animal ref = new Dog();
ref.ownerComesHome();
// ref.fetchBall(); // Compilation error
}
}
```

The ownerComesHome() method is accessible because it is declared in Animal. Java executes the overridden version from Dog. The fetchBall() method is declared only in Dog. It cannot be called directly through the Animal reference. This is an important limitation of using a parent-class reference: it provides access to the methods exposed by the parent type, not every method declared in the child class.

Answer: Upcasting means treating a child-class object through a reference of its parent type. For example:

```java
Dog d = new Dog();
Animal ref = d;
```

Here, d refers to a Dog object. The same object is then assigned to ref, whose declared type is Animal. Upcasting is permitted implicitly when the child class is compatible with the parent type. No explicit cast is required. The object remains a Dog object. Only the reference used to access it is declared using the parent type.

Answer: Downcasting means converting a parent-class reference to a reference of a child-class type. For example:

```java
Animal ref = new Dog();
Dog d = (Dog) ref;
d.fetchBall();
```

Here, ref is declared as Animal, but it refers to a Dog object. The expression (Dog) ref explicitly casts the reference to Dog. After the cast, the variable d can access methods available through the Dog type, including fetchBall(). Downcasting is valid only when the actual object is compatible with the target child type. If the object is not an instance of that type, Java throws a ClassCastException at runtime.

Answer: A specialized method can be accessed by downcasting the parent reference to the appropriate child type, provided the actual object belongs to that child class. For example:

```java
class Animal {
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Animal responds to the owner.");
}
}
class Dog extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog welcomes the owner.");
}
```

void fetchBall() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog fetches the ball.");
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Animal ref = new Dog();
ref.ownerComesHome();
Dog d = (Dog) ref;
d.fetchBall();
}
}
```

Output: Dog welcomes the owner. Dog fetches the ball. The parent reference can call the overridden method. To call fetchBall(), the reference is explicitly cast to Dog. Downcasting should be used only when the actual object is compatible with the child type. Otherwise, the cast may fail at runtime.

Answer: Upcasting converts a child-type reference into a parent-type reference. It is generally implicit and allows an object to be accessed through the parent type. Example:

```java
Dog d = new Dog();
Animal ref = d;
```

Downcasting converts a parent-type reference into a child-type reference. It requires an explicit cast and is safe only when the actual object is compatible with the target child type. Example:

```java
Animal ref = new Dog();
Dog d = (Dog) ref;
```

Upcasting supports working with different child objects through a common parent type. Downcasting is useful when child-specific functionality is required but is not available through the parent reference.

Answer: Implicit casting occurs when Java performs a permitted conversion automatically. Explicit casting requires the programmer to specify the intended conversion. For example, with primitive data types:

```java
int a = 10;
double b = a;
```

Java automatically converts the integer value to a double. This is an example of implicit widening conversion. An explicit narrowing conversion can be written as:

```java
double x = 10.75;
int y = (int) x;
```

Here, the cast explicitly converts the double value to int. The fractional part is discarded, so y becomes 10. Reference casting follows the rules of the inheritance hierarchy. Upcasting is generally implicit, while downcasting requires an explicit cast.

Answer: A parent reference can refer to different child objects at different points in a program. For example: Animal ref;

```java
ref = new Dog();
ref.ownerComesHome();
ref = new Cat();
ref.ownerComesHome();
```

When ref refers to a Dog object, the overridden method in Dog executes. When ref refers to a Cat object, the overridden method in Cat executes. The reference variable remains of type Animal. The actual object changes, and Java selects the corresponding overridden implementation at runtime. This demonstrates how a single parent reference can work with multiple child objects and support runtime polymorphism.
